      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τοσοῦτον ἀναφθὲν πῦρ σβέσαι",
          "english_quote": "extinguish so great a fire once it has been kindled",
          "issue": "The participle describes the fire as kindled. Following 'will be able,' the draft's 'once it has been kindled' can instead suggest a future condition. The note correctly describes an already-ignited fire, but the main wording leaves that state less clear than the Greek.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace the phrase with 'extinguish so great a fire that has been kindled,' preserving the description of the existing fire rather than introducing a potentially prospective condition.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀλλὰ τοῦτο οὕτως ἔχειν διαβεβαιοῦται ἕκαστος",
          "english_quote": "rather, everyone is firmly convinced that it is so.",
          "issue": "διαβεβαιοῦται with its accusative-and-infinitive complement expresses emphatic affirmation: each person maintains that matters are so. Rendering it as inward conviction collapses the distinction between the preceding absence of doubt and the ensuing positive assertion. The note acknowledges emphatic affirmation, but its appeal to the middle form does not establish the proposed inward-state rendering in this construction.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use 'rather, each person emphatically maintains that it is so.' Revise the note to explain the assertive force of διαβεβαιοῦται and its distinction from ἐνδοιάζει in the preceding clause. The replacement remains subject to fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
